Renders expanded stenotype entries to LSP snippet syntax for editor-side expansion (tabstops instead of cursor movement). Non-terminal partials strip brackets and drop tabstops; terminals map landings to LSP tabstops renumbered by tab order (lowest landing → `${1}`, highest → `${0}` final exit).

The inline Plover embedding wraps the snippet body between `@@` sentinels and uses `plover_inline_escape()` to double backslashes and escape braces so Plover's own typing reproduces them literally; real newlines become `\u{2424}` because a literal `\n` in the Plover format means "press Enter" and would split the span across buffer lines before the nvim plugin sees it. Both sentinel values and the newline marker must stay in sync with the nvim plugin.

`render_snippet(entry: &TypedEntry) -> Result<SnippetEntry, SnippetError>` dispatches to `render_terminal()` or `render_non_terminal()` based on `entry.terminal`. Terminals emit `Chunk::Landing(n)` as `${tab_index(n, sorted)}` where `tab_index()` returns 0 for the max landing and 1..k ascending for the rest; non-terminals strip brackets from `Chunk::Lit` text (via `strip_brackets()`) and drop landing chunks entirely. Both reject unresolved chunks. `wrap_inline_body()` formats the final body as `{^}@@<escaped>@@{^}` for Plover to type as a single keystroke.